WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.842
A console says: malware found.

2
00:00:02.842 --> 00:00:03.900
Is it really?

3
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.505
Today we check things ourselves.

4
00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:13.180
In this demonstration I verify three detections from the school using file hashes.

5
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:20.100
A hash is a fingerprint of a file: change one byte and the whole fingerprint changes.

6
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.112
Computing a hash only reads the file.

7
00:00:23.112 --> 00:00:24.496
It never runs it.

8
00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:35.459
First, what the console reported: the detection ID, the host, the threat name, and the SHA-256 hash the console saw.

9
00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:40.295
Now I compute the hashes myself with sha256sum.

10
00:00:40.295 --> 00:00:47.767
Remember, in this lab the samples are harmless text files that stand in for the real programs.

11
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:49.935
Compare them.

12
00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:55.189
DV-1 starts with one six f three seven: the same as the console.

13
00:00:55.189 --> 00:00:59.130
DV-2 starts with seven zero nine zero: the same.

14
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:06.109
But DV-3 starts with two zero five one on disk, while the console saw zero e a seven.

15
00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:09.311
The file changed after it was detected.

16
00:01:09.311 --> 00:01:17.932
That is a hash mismatch: I cannot trust the old detection, so I record it and escalate it for a re-check.

17
00:01:19.031 --> 00:01:25.494
Next, I look up the hashes that match in the threat intelligence list.

18
00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:31.310
DV-1, one six f three seven, is there: Ransom Win32 Crypt nine.

19
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.525
A known ransomware file.

20
00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:35.280
Verified malicious.

21
00:01:36.379 --> 00:01:40.211
DV-2, seven zero nine zero: grep prints nothing.

22
00:01:40.211 --> 00:01:47.875
It is not in the threat intelligence list, and the school has no known-good entry for it either.

23
00:01:47.875 --> 00:01:55.219
I cannot prove it is bad or good, so the SOP says: unknown, escalate to Tier 2 for analysis.

24
00:01:56.319 --> 00:01:58.316
Here are my three verdicts.

25
00:01:58.316 --> 00:02:05.487
Three detections, three different answers, and none of them came from trusting the console alone.

26
00:02:06.587 --> 00:02:07.387
Your turn.

27
00:02:07.387 --> 00:02:11.707
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 8 and Activity 8.1.

28
00:02:11.707 --> 00:02:14.108
Five detections from the bank.

29
00:02:14.108 --> 00:02:17.628
Hash every sample, compare with the console,

30
00:02:17.628 --> 00:02:24.908
then look each hash up in the threat intelligence list and in the vendor's known-good list.

31
00:02:24.908 --> 00:02:27.468
One of them is a false positive.

